{
  "image_name": "b2626482790549d849e7dcd37f581808.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major electrode problems in chloride-containing electrolysis and the strategies used to overcome them. On the anode side, chloride ions can be oxidized to chlorine or hypochlorite and the electrode itself can corrode. On the cathode side, dissolved ions such as Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺ can deposit as insoluble precipitates, while chloride also contributes to corrosion. Together these sketches frame the challenge of running electrolysis in seawater-like media.\n\nThe rest of the figure shows several solution routes. Physical regulation uses protective surface layers to block chloride access. Chemical regulation adjusts the catalyst’s active sites so chloride adsorption is suppressed. A hybrid electrolysis concept replaces the oxygen evolution reaction with an alternative anodic oxidation, lowering the required anodic potential below the usual water-oxidation value while pairing it with hydrogen evolution at the cathode. Additional design strategies include creating 3D nanostructured electrodes, tuning electronic structure through doping and interface engineering, and regulating the local microenvironment near the surface to repel chloride while favoring hydroxide-related reactions. Overall, the figure presents a toolbox for improving selectivity, stability, and resistance to fouling and corrosion in saline electrolysis systems.",
